WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.480
The question is, how do we pass data down to our components?

00:03.480 --> 00:06.920
So this is where we talk about the idea and it's called props,

00:06.920 --> 00:08.960
which is shorthand for properties.

00:08.960 --> 00:12.400
The reason why I like the concept of props a lot is because if you think about it,

00:12.400 --> 00:15.080
they're really just data attributes on your HTML element.

00:15.080 --> 00:16.720
But it sounds really fancy when you say props,

00:16.720 --> 00:19.480
but really it's just you're passing an attribute a value,

00:19.480 --> 00:22.080
and then that component can access that attribute.

00:22.080 --> 00:23.200
That's really what it is.

00:23.200 --> 00:25.200
So I think of props as custom attributes.

00:25.200 --> 00:27.720
Well, what do we need? We need the character list.

00:27.880 --> 00:32.080
So for now, I'm going to add a prop called characters,

00:32.080 --> 00:36.360
and this is going to be passing down our character list.

00:36.360 --> 00:40.080
So then I can go ahead and bind it to character list like this.

00:40.080 --> 00:43.400
Then does anyone know what I'm missing here?

00:43.400 --> 00:46.680
colon, yep, because I need to v-bind this to make it dynamic.

00:46.680 --> 00:49.320
So I'm going to just do the shorthand for colon.

00:49.320 --> 00:51.120
But that's not good enough though,

00:51.120 --> 00:55.040
because after all, what we just did was we just passed it in data.

00:55.080 --> 00:57.960
But one of the things I really like about view,

00:57.960 --> 00:59.920
when it comes, it's not view specific,

00:59.920 --> 01:02.560
but I did like this when I started learning about it,

01:02.560 --> 01:05.480
is that it's really all about trying to document your components

01:05.480 --> 01:07.280
and making it easier for people to use.

01:07.280 --> 01:09.280
So inside of your options, you have the ability

01:09.280 --> 01:12.160
to define what props you're going to get, right?

01:12.160 --> 01:14.120
So actually, before I do the object one,

01:14.120 --> 01:15.880
the simplest way to define your props

01:15.880 --> 01:17.240
is through an array of strings.

01:17.240 --> 01:19.800
So I could just be like characters like that.

01:21.080 --> 01:24.320
And then you can see here, let me save that, okay?

01:24.320 --> 01:28.680
What this means now is that if I do this.characters,

01:28.680 --> 01:30.960
view will know this character's reference

01:30.960 --> 01:32.560
refers to this prop up here.

01:33.640 --> 01:36.320
And so if I save this now and refresh,

01:36.320 --> 01:39.360
you'll notice our statistics suddenly works again.

01:39.360 --> 01:41.920
Now the thing is though, I only show you this

01:41.920 --> 01:43.480
because in case you see this in the wild,

01:43.480 --> 01:47.240
this is code smell, because again, characters as a string

01:47.240 --> 01:49.720
doesn't tell you very much about what you expect as a prop.

01:49.720 --> 01:51.840
If you're interested in enterprise level patterns

01:51.840 --> 01:52.680
and that kind of stuff,

01:52.680 --> 01:55.360
be sure to check out the production grade view

01:55.360 --> 01:56.400
that I recorded before.

01:56.400 --> 01:58.120
And so that covers a lot of the different patterns

01:58.120 --> 01:59.520
that you'll see in enterprise apps.

01:59.520 --> 02:01.880
And so this is kind of like a snippet

02:01.880 --> 02:03.720
of something that I mentioned.

02:03.720 --> 02:05.400
Okay, so when it comes to props,

02:05.400 --> 02:07.300
I recommend defining it via object.

02:07.300 --> 02:08.140
Why?

02:08.140 --> 02:10.360
Because what we can do is for characters,

02:10.360 --> 02:12.800
we can do things like define what kind of type

02:12.800 --> 02:14.000
that we're expecting.

02:14.000 --> 02:16.120
And again, this is not type and typescript perspective.

02:16.120 --> 02:17.960
This is more of a runtime check,

02:17.960 --> 02:20.720
like view helps you out when you pass something funny down.

02:20.720 --> 02:21.800
It'll just kind of highlight like,

02:21.800 --> 02:23.680
hey, you told me you wanted this.

02:23.680 --> 02:25.120
Why is it something different?

02:25.120 --> 02:27.640
So in this case, I know it's just gonna be an array.

02:27.640 --> 02:30.640
More importantly, I can say this is a required property.

02:31.960 --> 02:35.120
Or I could say, I could also define

02:35.120 --> 02:37.240
a default property on it as well.

02:37.240 --> 02:38.400
But for this particular case,

02:38.400 --> 02:40.080
all we need to worry about is the fact that

02:40.080 --> 02:42.240
it's a required property, and that's important

02:42.240 --> 02:44.120
because what you're doing here

02:44.120 --> 02:46.040
is basically self-documenting the component

02:46.040 --> 02:49.040
so that people in the future can actually use it correctly.

02:50.040 --> 02:51.800
And so when I save this here,

02:51.800 --> 02:53.920
you see that we're refreshed, this is good.

02:53.920 --> 02:55.200
But then when we go back to app.view,

02:55.200 --> 02:56.800
let's say I accidentally tried to use

02:56.800 --> 02:59.760
Bender statistics without passing the component.

02:59.760 --> 03:01.400
Let's see if we'll let you yell at me.

03:02.640 --> 03:04.600
Bum bum bum bum bum.

03:04.600 --> 03:06.080
So this time it doesn't yell at me.

03:06.080 --> 03:08.520
But there are times where I think,

03:08.520 --> 03:10.520
actually let me do this real quick.

03:10.520 --> 03:15.280
If I do characters equals one, two, three.

03:16.240 --> 03:19.080
Save.

03:19.080 --> 03:21.560
Looks like it's not doing any checks for me at the moment.

03:21.560 --> 03:22.880
It's being kind of finicky.

03:22.880 --> 03:24.800
But anyhow, at the bare minimum,

03:24.800 --> 03:26.920
when you're doing this, you can actually see

03:26.920 --> 03:28.880
it's easy to read, easy to understand,

03:28.880 --> 03:30.120
and then more importantly,

03:30.120 --> 03:31.920
makes your app easier to maintain.

03:31.920 --> 03:34.600
So once again, we are a character list like this.

03:35.480 --> 03:36.320
Oops.

03:37.640 --> 03:38.480
Okay.

03:39.720 --> 03:42.560
And so props really is basically the glue

03:42.560 --> 03:44.680
to which we can connect our parent components

03:44.680 --> 03:46.720
into our child components.

03:46.720 --> 03:47.600
There's a question though?

03:47.600 --> 03:49.000
Functions as props.

03:50.960 --> 03:51.800
Okay.

03:51.800 --> 03:54.960
So this is a hotly-divated one.

03:54.960 --> 03:55.800
Okay.

03:55.800 --> 03:57.480
So the question here is around the concept

03:57.480 --> 03:59.000
of functions as props.

03:59.000 --> 04:02.240
So in other words, to set up the example here,

04:02.240 --> 04:05.720
let me go into the sandbox one instead, just to show.

04:07.360 --> 04:08.920
Okay.

04:08.920 --> 04:11.920
So let's say in base counter, we had a props.

04:11.920 --> 04:13.840
And this is actually something that I think

04:13.880 --> 04:16.040
is more common in React code bases.

04:16.960 --> 04:21.960
So in app.view, we saw that we actually

04:22.280 --> 04:23.920
actually scoped the increment counter

04:23.920 --> 04:25.600
directly to the component, right?

04:25.600 --> 04:28.680
Well, theoretically, what you could do

04:28.680 --> 04:31.680
is with this concept is that you would have

04:31.680 --> 04:33.840
your methods at your parent component.

04:33.840 --> 04:37.160
So it'd be at this level, right?

04:37.160 --> 04:39.240
And you'd have this increment function.

04:39.240 --> 04:42.160
And then what you pass down to the component

04:42.160 --> 04:43.880
is the actual increment function.

04:43.880 --> 04:47.720
So it would look something like this, right?

04:47.720 --> 04:49.920
And then in terms of how this would be,

04:49.920 --> 04:52.080
how it would look from a component perspective,

04:52.080 --> 04:55.000
you would have this increment prop,

04:55.000 --> 04:56.680
and it's like type function, right?

04:56.680 --> 04:59.680
And then just be like, oops, required true.

05:00.760 --> 05:02.480
There's an example of a props.

05:03.640 --> 05:06.480
Personally, I am not a fan of passing functions down

05:06.480 --> 05:07.320
as props.

05:07.320 --> 05:10.080
The reason for this is because I know that,

05:10.080 --> 05:11.720
especially when we are programming,

05:11.720 --> 05:14.800
we get caught up in trying to do factory functions

05:14.800 --> 05:16.320
and with factory components,

05:16.320 --> 05:18.800
where it becomes very easy to genericize everything.

05:18.800 --> 05:20.680
And so that's why I think one of the reasons

05:20.680 --> 05:22.440
it even got popular to pass functions down

05:22.440 --> 05:24.120
is because you're basically saying,

05:24.120 --> 05:26.760
as a parent, I'm gonna tell the child what to run.

05:27.840 --> 05:29.440
But what I found with that a lot of times

05:29.440 --> 05:32.680
is that now you're starting to kind of conflate the,

05:32.680 --> 05:35.000
like I feel like the parent starts to do too much work

05:35.000 --> 05:38.000
in terms of like, if the scope of the child

05:38.000 --> 05:40.040
is meant to increment,

05:40.040 --> 05:42.080
I found very few times where it didn't make sense

05:42.080 --> 05:45.280
for the child to own that from the beginning,

05:45.280 --> 05:47.080
as far as like functionality-wise.

05:47.880 --> 05:50.680
So in this example, I tend to think about this

05:50.680 --> 05:55.400
in the add new airbender form.

05:55.400 --> 05:59.560
Like the parent app owns the list of airbenders,

05:59.560 --> 06:03.800
and the form has the what is a new airbender

06:03.800 --> 06:05.360
and how do I modify it.

06:05.400 --> 06:08.560
But then when I wanna actually add that to that list,

06:08.560 --> 06:11.320
like I could modify the list in the child,

06:11.320 --> 06:13.600
but that feels dirtier than having the parent

06:13.600 --> 06:15.640
own that function that actually adds it to the list.

06:15.640 --> 06:16.760
I love this question.

06:16.760 --> 06:20.920
So that is actually a good lead-in after the exercise.

06:22.480 --> 06:26.840
I'll call it what I consider the more standard pattern

06:26.840 --> 06:27.680
of dealing with that.

06:27.680 --> 06:30.680
And so to recap, in case anyone couldn't hear,

06:30.680 --> 06:33.880
is that let's go back to the example brought up

06:33.880 --> 06:37.440
was inside of my avatar airbender component.

06:37.440 --> 06:39.320
Earlier, you might have heard me say, for example,

06:39.320 --> 06:41.800
that the component hit a limit, and there's a reason for that.

06:41.800 --> 06:44.800
But for the favorite character list, as was mentioned,

06:44.800 --> 06:47.400
if we were to break out favorite character right here

06:48.360 --> 06:52.000
into its own component, it's tricky because at this point,

06:52.000 --> 06:55.040
favorite list is also dependent on like,

06:56.680 --> 07:00.280
there's like the data exists in the parent.

07:00.280 --> 07:02.120
And so you don't necessarily wanna pass the data

07:02.120 --> 07:04.520
into the child for the child to modify.

07:04.520 --> 07:07.400
But at the same time, then it sounds like it would be better

07:07.400 --> 07:09.440
to then pass a function to the child

07:09.440 --> 07:11.280
to tell it what to do, right?

07:11.280 --> 07:13.560
But I'll leave that a little bit on the cliffhanger,

07:13.560 --> 07:15.480
but just know that there is a better way

07:15.480 --> 07:17.000
than passing functions down as props

07:17.000 --> 07:18.400
when it comes to that scenario.

